      "events": [],
      "expressionProperties": {},
      "value": "When interacting with web pages, a series of user actions and browser responses trigger various events. <b>click</b> is the most common, firing when a user presses and releases a mouse button on an element. Related to this are mouse events: <b>mousedown</b> fires when the mouse button is pressed down, <b>mouseup</b> when it's released, and <b>mousemove</b> fires repeatedly as the mouse pointer is moved over an element. The events <b>mouseover</b> and <b>mouseout</b> fire when the pointer enters and leaves an element, respectively. For keyboard interactions, <b>keydown</b> fires when a key is pressed down, <b>keyup</b> when it's released, and <b>keypress</b> for most printable characters. On the other hand, <b>focus</b> and <b>blur</b> events deal with an element's active state: focus fires when an element becomes active, such as when you click inside a text box, while blur fires when it loses that active state. Lastly, the <b>change</b> event is triggered when an element's value, like a form input, is altered and the user moves away from it.",
      "dynamicValueAccessor": null
    }
  ],
